            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"In Christian teaching, 'meat' (or 'solid food') primarily symbolizes advanced spiritual teaching or deep doctrine suited for mature believers, in contrast to 'milk' which represents elementary teaching. An alternative meaning recognizes 'meat' as doing the will of God, based on Jesus' words in John 4:34.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines 'meat' as the word obeyed to completeness or 'the word done.' It unifies the biblical usages by arguing that milk is the word merely received, while meat is the word actively obeyed and practiced.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Advanced spiritual teaching or deep doctrine (versus elementary teaching).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The word obeyed to completeness; action and obedience (the word done, versus the word merely received).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus treats Hebrews 5 and 1 Corinthians 3 as references to advanced theological instruction, separate from the obedience-oriented 'meat' of John 4. The book applies the John 4 definition universally, claiming the 'strong meat' of Hebrews 5 is actually the practice of the word.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus believes the milk/meat distinction is a contrast between basic and advanced levels of teaching or doctrine. The book contradicts this by asserting the distinction is between hearing the word (milk) and doing the word (meat).\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book rejects the primary consensus definition (advanced doctrine) by elevating a recognized alternative (doing God's will) and retrofitting the primary consensus passages (such as Hebrews 5) to fit this obedience-based definition. This constitutes a material contradiction of the consensus's core referent.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In Hebrews 5:11-14 and 1 Corinthians 3:1-2, the context explicitly focuses on the audience's inability to understand or receive spoken instruction ('dull of hearing,' 'not able to bear it'), which strongly supports the consensus view that the meat in those passages refers to advanced teaching rather than the act of obedience itself.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
